  "modules": [
    {
      "cards": [
        {
          "front": "How many rows in a truth table with 4 variables?",
          "back": "2^4 = **16** rows \u2014 one per assignment."
        },
        {
          "front": "State De Morgan's law for AND.",
          "back": "NOT (p AND q) = (NOT p) OR (NOT q) \u2014 verified on all 4 rows."
        },
        {
          "front": "State De Morgan's law for OR.",
          "back": "NOT (p OR q) = (NOT p) AND (NOT q)."
        },
        {
          "front": "Define p -> q without using ->.",
          "back": "(NOT p) OR q. Only false when p=T, q=F."
        },
        {
          "front": "What is the contrapositive of p -> q?",
          "back": "(NOT q) -> (NOT p) \u2014 logically equivalent to the original."
        },
        {
          "front": "Is the converse of p -> q equivalent to p -> q?",
          "back": "No. Counterexample: p=T, q=F makes p->q false but q->p true."
        },
        {
          "front": "Absorption law?",
          "back": "p OR (p AND q) = p; p AND (p OR q) = p."
        },
        {
          "front": "What does EXISTS x, P(x) require over a finite domain?",
          "back": "At least one domain element making P true \u2014 a single witness."
        },
        {
          "front": "What does FORALL x, P(x) require?",
          "back": "Every domain element makes P true \u2014 one counterexample falsifies it."
        },
        {
          "front": "Simplify (a AND b) OR (a AND NOT b).",
          "back": "a \u2014 factor to a AND (b OR NOT b) = a AND T."
        },
        {
          "front": "Order of evaluation in (p AND q) OR (NOT r)?",
          "back": "Parentheses, then NOT, then AND, then OR \u2014 inside-out."
        },
        {
          "front": "Evaluate (T AND F) OR (NOT T).",
          "back": "(F) OR (F) = **F**."
        }
      ],
      "code": "IL-01",
      "crossLinks": [],
      "drills": [
        {
          "title": "Logic evaluation sprint",
          "items": [
            {
              "prompt": "Evaluate (T OR F) AND (NOT F).",
              "answer": "T",
              "explain": "(T OR F)=T; NOT F=T; T AND T=T."
            },
            {
              "prompt": "Evaluate NOT (T AND T) OR F.",
              "answer": "F",
              "explain": "NOT(T AND T)=F; F OR F=F. NOT binds tightest."
            },
            {
              "prompt": "p=F, q=F. Evaluate (NOT p) -> q.",
              "answer": "F",
              "explain": "(NOT F)=T; T -> F is the one false case of implication."
            },
            {
              "prompt": "How many assignments falsify (p AND q) OR (p AND NOT q)?",
              "answer": "2 of 4",
              "explain": "The expression simplifies to p; falsified exactly when p=F (2 rows)."
            }
          ],
          "id": "il-01-esoteric-foundations-math-logic--drill-1"
        },
        {
          "title": "Equivalence proofs",
          "items": [
            {
              "prompt": "Prove NOT (p OR q) = (NOT p) AND (NOT q) by cases.",
              "answer": "All 4 rows agree",
              "explain": "Enumerate (T,T),(T,F),(F,T),(F,F): both sides match on every row \u2014 De Morgan verified."
            },
            {
              "prompt": "Is (p -> q) equivalent to (NOT p) -> (NOT q)? Justify.",
              "answer": "No",
              "explain": "Counterexample p=T,q=T: original true, inverse (F->F) true... use p=F,q=T: original true, inverse (T->F) false. Not equivalent."
            },
            {
              "prompt": "Simplify (x OR y) AND (x OR NOT y) AND (NOT x OR y).",
              "answer": "x AND y",
              "explain": "First two factor to x; x AND ((NOT x) OR y) absorbs to x AND y."
            },
            {
              "prompt": "Translate 'every admin has MFA' into quantifiers.",
              "answer": "FORALL u, (admin(u) -> mfa(u))",
              "explain": "Universal over users; implication inside captures 'if admin then MFA'."
            }
          ],
          "id": "il-01-esoteric-foundations-math-logic--drill-2"
        }
      ],
      "estimatedMinutes": 345,
      "handsOnLab": {
        "title": "Verify logic laws in Python",
        "brief": "You will write a brute-force truth-table checker in Python and use it to verify De Morgan's laws, the contrapositive, and absorption \u2014 replacing hand-waving with enumeration.",
        "steps": [
          "Write a function tt(vars, fn) that iterates over all 2^n assignments using itertools.product and returns the table.",
          "Use it to print the full truth table of (p -> q), defined as (not p) or q.",
          "Verify De Morgan: assert all rows agree for NOT(p AND q) vs (NOT p) OR (NOT q).",
          "Verify the contrapositive: assert (p->q) matches ((not q) -> (not p)) on all rows, and find the counterexample row for the converse.",
          "Verify absorption p OR (p AND q) == p on all 8 rows of (p,q,r) \u2014 extra variable included to prove irrelevance.",
          "Write verify_equiv(f, g, vars) and use it to check two simplifications of your own invention."
        ],
        "deliverable": "logic_check.py plus a short report: which laws verified, the converse counterexample row, and your two invented equivalences with verdicts.",
        "checkpoint": "All asserts pass; the report names the exact assignment falsifying the converse.",
        "id": "il-01-esoteric-foundations-math-logic--lab"
      },
      "id": "il-01-esoteric-foundations-math-logic",
      "mockTask": {
        "q": "Which single law turns NOT (a AND b AND c) into a disjunction?",
        "choices": [
          "De Morgan's law",
          "Absorption",
          "Double negation",
          "Idempotence"
        ],
        "answer": 0,
        "explain": "NOT (a AND b AND c) = (NOT a) OR (NOT b) OR (NOT c) by repeated De Morgan.",
        "id": "il-01-esoteric-foundations-math-logic--mock",
        "type": "choice"
      },
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Propositions and truth values",
          "body": "A proposition is a statement that is either true or false, never both. `p AND q` is true only when both are true; `p OR q` is true when at least one is true; `NOT p` flips the value. Worked example: evaluate `(p AND q) OR (NOT r)` at p=T, q=F, r=T. First `p AND q` = T AND F = F. Then `NOT r` = NOT T = F. Finally `F OR F` = **F**. So the whole expression is **F**. Work inside-out: parentheses first, then NOT, then AND, then OR \u2014 the same precedence habit that prevents bugs in code conditions."
        },
        {
          "title": "Truth tables, computed by enumeration",
          "body": "A truth table lists every assignment of the variables. With n variables there are 2^n rows \u2014 for n=3 that is **8** rows, computed as 2^3. Worked example: the implication `p -> q`, defined as `(NOT p) OR q`. Its table: p=T,q=T -> T; p=T,q=F -> F; p=F,q=T -> T; p=F,q=F -> T. The only false row is true-premise/false-conclusion. Enumerating all rows mechanically (as the lab does in Python) removes all doubt: no case is forgotten, because the count 2^n proves the table is complete."
        },
        {
          "title": "De Morgan's laws, verified by brute force",
          "body": "De Morgan's laws: `NOT (p AND q)` equals `(NOT p) OR (NOT q)`, and `NOT (p OR q)` equals `(NOT p) AND (NOT q)`. Worked verification: checking all 4 assignments of (p,q) in Python, every row agrees \u2014 **True**. This is the law behind simplifying negated conditions: `NOT (admin AND active)` becomes `NOT admin OR NOT active`. In code and in firewall/policy rules, pushing NOT inward with De Morgan turns an unreadable negated conjunction into a plain disjunction."
        },
        {
          "title": "Implication and the contrapositive",
          "body": "`p -> q` (if p then q) is logically equivalent to its contrapositive `(NOT q) -> (NOT p)`. Worked verification: all 4 assignments agree \u2014 **True**. The converse `q -> p` is NOT equivalent (check p=T,q=F: implication false, converse true). Practical use: to prove 'if the build passes then tests ran', prove the contrapositive 'if tests did not run then the build fails' \u2014 often the easier direction. Confusing implication with converse is one of the most common logic errors in specifications."
        },
        {
          "title": "Predicate logic over finite domains",
          "body": "Predicates add quantifiers: `FORALL x, P(x)` and `EXISTS x, P(x)`. Over a finite domain you can evaluate them by enumeration. Worked example: domain = {2, 3, 4}, statement `EXISTS x, x is prime AND x > 3`. Check: x=2 (prime, not >3, fails), x=3 (prime, not >3, fails), x=4 (not prime, fails). No witness exists, so the statement is **false**. Change `>3` to `>=3`: x=3 is prime and >= 3, so the statement becomes **true**. Existence claims need exactly one witness; universal claims need every case. This discipline maps directly onto testing: 'there exists an input that crashes' vs 'for all inputs it terminates'."
        },
        {
          "title": "Boolean algebra for systems work",
          "body": "Boolean algebra gives simplification laws: absorption `p OR (p AND q) = p`, distribution, idempotence `p AND p = p`. Worked example: simplify `(admin AND active) OR (admin AND NOT active)`. Factor admin: `admin AND (active OR NOT active)` = `admin AND T` = **admin**. Verified by enumerating all 4 assignments of (admin, active): both sides agree on every row. Why it matters: access-policy engines evaluate exactly these expressions. Simplifying before deploying means fewer rules, faster evaluation, and policies a human can audit. Formal logic is the machinery for tiering claims without fooling yourself: valid argument forms keep DOCUMENTED separate from TRADITIONAL."
        }
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Evaluate compound propositions by precedence, inside-out.",
        "Build and read truth tables; prove completeness via the 2^n count.",
        "Apply De Morgan's laws and verify equivalences by brute-force enumeration.",
        "Distinguish implication, converse, and contrapositive \u2014 and know which are equivalent."
      ],
      "prerequisiteFor": [
        "il01-hermetic-foundations",
        "il01-dee-enochian",
        "il01-ccru-seminar"
