          "11",
          "5",
          "23",
          "1"
        ],
        "answer": 0,
        "explain": "5\u00d723 = 115 \u2261 11 (mod 26).",
        "id": "il-01-esoteric-foundations-math-numtheory--mock",
        "type": "choice"
      },
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Divisibility and primes",
          "body": "d divides n if n = d\u00b7k for integer k. A prime has exactly two divisors. Worked example: factor 180. 180 = 2^2 \u00d7 3^2 \u00d7 5 (verify: 4\u00d79\u00d75 = 180). Primality testing by trial division only needs divisors up to \u221an: to test 97, check primes \u2264 \u221a97 \u2248 9.8, i.e. 2,3,5,7 \u2014 none divide, so 97 is prime. Unique prime factorization is the foundation everything below builds on."
        },
        {
          "title": "GCD and the Euclidean algorithm",
          "body": "Worked example: gcd(48, 180). Euclid: 180 = 3\u00d748 + 36; 48 = 1\u00d736 + 12; 36 = 3\u00d712 + 0. Last nonzero remainder: **12**. Python: math.gcd(48,180) = 12 \u2014 agreement. The extended algorithm also finds integers x,y with 48x+180y=gcd; those coefficients are exactly what the next note's inverse computation uses. GCD answers 'what's the largest shared structure' \u2014 from simplifying fractions to RSA key generation."
        },
        {
          "title": "Modular arithmetic",
          "body": "a \u2261 b (mod m) means m divides a\u2212b. Worked examples mod 26: 17 + 10 = 27 \u2261 1; 17 \u00d7 3 = 51 \u2261 25; 100 mod 26 = **22** since 26\u00d73=78 and 100\u221278=22. Addition, subtraction, multiplication all work mod m; division needs an inverse, which exists exactly when gcd(a,m)=1. Here gcd(17,26) = 1, so 17 has an inverse mod 26. Number theory also carries the historical thread of this course: number lore belongs to the TRADITIONAL tier unless primary sources say otherwise."
        },
        {
          "title": "Modular inverses, computed",
          "body": "Worked example: inverse of 17 mod 26. Computed: 17^(-1) mod 26 = **23**. Verification: 17\u00d723 = 391 = 26\u00d715 + 1, so 17\u00d723 \u2261 **1** (mod 26) \u2713. Solve 17x \u2261 5 (mod 26): multiply by the inverse, x \u2261 5\u00d723 = 115 \u2261 **11** (mod 26). Check: 17\u00d711 = 187 \u2261 5 \u2713. Inverses turn modular division into multiplication \u2014 the operation RSA decryption performs."
        },
        {
          "title": "Euler's totient",
          "body": "\u03c6(m) counts integers 1..m coprime to m. Worked example: \u03c6(26). Counting k with gcd(k,26)=1 gives **12**. Euler's theorem: if gcd(a,m)=1 then a^\u03c6(m) \u2261 1 (mod m). Check with a=3, m=26: \u03c6=12, 3^12 mod 26 = **1** \u2713. This theorem is why RSA decryption works: exponents reduce mod \u03c6(n)."
        },
        {
          "title": "Toy RSA, end to end",
          "body": "Worked example with small primes p=61, q=53. n = 61\u00d753 = **3233**; \u03c6(n) = 60\u00d752 = **3120**. Choose public exponent e=17 (coprime to \u03c6). Private exponent d = e^(\u22121) mod \u03c6 = **2753**. Encrypt message 42: c = 42^17 mod 3233 = **2557**. Decrypt: m = 2557^2753 mod 3233 = **42** \u2014 the original message \u2713. Every number here was computed, not asserted. Real RSA uses 2048-bit primes, but the mathematics is exactly this."
        }
      ],
