      "handsOnLab": {
        "id": "il01-hermetic-foundations--lab",
        "title": "Source-tiering dossier: Hermetic Foundations",
        "brief": "You will build a tiered evidence dossier for Hermetic Foundations: selecting claims from the module, assigning each an explicit epistemic tier, and grounding every tier in primary sources. This turns passive reading into scholarly practice.",
        "steps": [
          "Select three substantive claims from this module's notes on Hermetic Foundations.",
          "For each claim, locate the primary source it rests on (the actual text, in translation if needed) and record the exact passage.",
          "Assign each claim a tier: DOCUMENTED, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS, TRADITIONAL, or CONTESTED \u2014 with a one-paragraph justification for the tier.",
          "Find one credible source that disagrees with or complicates each claim; summarize the counter-position fairly.",
          "Write a 400-word synthesis: what survives tiering, what doesn't, and what remains genuinely unknown.",
          "Peer-review your own dossier after 48 hours: flag every place where belief crept past evidence and correct it."
        ],
        "deliverable": "tiering-dossier.md containing the three tiered claims with primary-source passages, counter-positions, and the synthesis \u2014 every claim tiered, every tier justified.",
        "checkpoint": "Three claims, each with a tier, a primary-source passage, a counter-position, and a tier justification; the synthesis names what remains unknown."
      },
      "id": "il01-hermetic-foundations",
      "mockTask": {
        "id": "il01-hermetic-foundations--mock",
        "type": "choice",
        "q": "Casaubon's significance?",
        "choices": [
          "translated the Corpus",
          "founded the Golden Dawn",
          "wrote the Principia",
          "dated it late-antique via philology \u2014 founding act of esoteric historiography"
        ],
        "answer": 3,
        "explain": "1614: the texts are late-antique, not primeval \u2014 dates have consequences. (Concepts: Casaubon.)"
      },
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Concepts",
          "body": "- **Corpus Hermeticum** \u2014 the dialogues attributed to Hermes Trismegistus; late-antique Alexandrian texts, not primeval Egyptian scripture.\n- **Asclepius** \u2014 the most complete single Hermetic text; read it first.\n- **Nag Hammadi** \u2014 the 1945 find that reframed the world the Hermetic texts belong to.\n- **Late-antique Alexandria** \u2014 the syncretic city where Greek philosophy, Egyptian religion, and emerging Christianity met; the Corpus is what happens when they share a city.\n- **Casaubon (1614)** \u2014 dated the Corpus to late antiquity by philology; the founding act of esoteric historiography. Texts have dates, and dates have consequences.\n- **Cipher Manuscripts** \u2014 the Golden Dawn\u2019s founding documents: DOCUMENTED as artifacts, CONTESTED as to origin; tier each claim separately.\n- **Golden Dawn (1888\u20131903)** \u2014 the order of Westcott, Mathers, and Woodman, through the schisms; read its grade papers as a curriculum, not a mystery.\n- **Grade system** \u2014 the ladder from 0=0 upward; a syllabus: what\u2019s taught, in what order, to what end.\n- **LBRP** \u2014 the Lesser Banishing Ritual of the Pentagram; a Golden Dawn paper and our study object. Note what the papers say vs. what later books claim.\n- **Gardnerian Wicca** \u2014 Wicca\u2019s documented origins; tier the origin claims as you meet them.\n- **Thelema** \u2014 Crowley\u2019s current: A.\u2019.\u2019A.\u2019., O.T.O., and the Book of the Law.\n- **Book of the Law** \u2014 primary text first, Crowley\u2019s commentaries second, others\u2019 opinions last.\n- **Sigilization** \u2014 Austin Osman Spare\u2019s technique: DOCUMENTED as technique, CONTESTED as mechanism.\n- **Paradigm shifting** \u2014 Peter Carroll\u2019s chaos-magical move: belief as instrument, a tool rather than a truth-claim.\n- **Principia Discordia** \u2014 the Discordian scripture; chaos magic\u2019s irreverent cousin.\n- **Tree of Life** \u2014 the Hermetic Qabalah\u2019s map; a memory palace organizing planets, elements, and tarot, whatever else it is.\n- **Correspondences** \u2014 the Qabalistic system of linkages across planets, elements, and tarot.\n- **Paracelsus** \u2014 iatrochemistry: alchemy as early chemistry and medicine; \u201cthe dose makes the poison\u201d is alchemy\u2019s most DOCUMENTED contribution to science.\n- **DOCUMENTED** \u2014 primary sources, artifacts, dated texts: the bedrock tier.\n- **SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS** \u2014 what historians agree on: provisional, cited.\n- **TRADITIONAL** \u2014 what lineages claim about themselves: reported, not endorsed.\n- **CONTESTED** \u2014 open questions and rival readings: where the seminar lives."
        },
        {
          "title": "Hands-on procedure",
          "body": "Before we theorize, we practice the one reflex this academy exists to build: tiering a claim. You\u2019ll use the Tiering Engine lab (il01-lab-tier) \u2014 but the procedure is identical on paper.\n\n1. **Choose one claim** from this module\u2019s readings \u2014 for example, \u201cThe Corpus Hermeticum preserves primeval Egyptian wisdom.\u201d\n2. **Split it into atomic sub-claims:** (a) the manuscripts exist and date to late antiquity; (b) their content originates in primeval Egypt.\n3. **Collect the evidence you actually hold** for each sub-claim: the manuscript tradition and Casaubon\u2019s 1614 philology for (a); lineage assertion for (b).\n4. **Run each sub-claim** through the lab\u2019s tier_claim function with its evidence string, and read the (tier, justification) pairs it returns.\n5. **Record every pair in your tiered reading journal** with the evidence cited \u2014 the justification matters more than the label.\n6. **Circle the CONTESTED sub-claims.** Those are not failures; they are your seminar questions for the rest of the academy."
        },
        {
          "title": "Project brief",
          "body": "The brief. Choose one text from this module \u2014 the Asclepius, a Golden Dawn grade paper, or a passage of the Book of the Law \u2014 and produce a tiered dossier a historian would respect.\n\nDeliverables. (1) A claim inventory: every factual claim the text or its surrounding tradition makes about origins, authorship, and transmission. (2) A tier for each claim \u2014 DOCUMENTED, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS, TRADITIONAL, or CONTESTED \u2014 with the evidence cited. (3) One CONTESTED question argued both ways, about 300 words per side. (4) A reading-order recommendation for the next student: what to read first, and why.\n\nThe standard. Your tiers must survive cross-examination: TRADITIONAL is never marked wrong for being traditional, and DOCUMENTED is never awarded without evidence. Assessments test whether your dossier holds up \u2014 never what you believe."
        },
        {
          "title": "Honesty note",
          "body": "Original study material, written for this academy \u2014 the questions, labs, and scenarios here are authored, not transcribed from any order\u2019s papers. The four tier labels are our historiographic method, not verdicts on the traditions: TRADITIONAL means \u201cthe lineage claims this,\u201d reported accurately and labeled honestly \u2014 never endorsed, never mocked. \u201cThe lineage claims X\u201d is itself a DOCUMENTED fact about the claim, whatever X\u2019s own tier is. Interstitium Labs is not affiliated with any esoteric order and confers no credential; assessments test literacy \u2014 what the texts say, what tier applies, how scholars argue \u2014 never belief."
        },
        {
          "title": "Worked example: A text claims primeval Egyptian origins; philology dates it to 200\u2026",
          "body": "**Problem.** A text claims primeval Egyptian origins; philology dates it to 200 CE. Tier?\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud \u2014 in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** CONTESTED on origins; DOCUMENTED on the manuscript date\n- **Why:** Tier each claim separately \u2014 the manuscript\u2019s date is DOCUMENTED; the origin story is CONTESTED. (Concepts: the four tiers; Hands-on procedure, step 2.)\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Tier each claim separately \u2014 the manuscript\u2019s date is DOCUMENTED; the origin story is CONTESTED.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test \u2014 if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
          "body": "Esoteric material punishes sloppy epistemics harder than most subjects. These are the failure modes that separate scholars from storytellers in Hermetic Foundations:\n- **Collapsing the tiers.** The single most common failure: treating a TRADITIONAL claim as DOCUMENTED because it feels old. Every claim you touch in Hermetic Foundations gets an explicit tier \u2014 DOCUMENTED, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS, TRADITIONAL, or CONTESTED \u2014 before you use it.\n- **Anachronism.** Reading modern meanings back into period terms. When a Hermetic Foundations source says 'spirit', 'energy', or 'science', find what the word meant to the author, not what it means on social media.\n- **Single-source trust.** One grimoire, one translation, one blogger is not a consensus. Corroborate across at least two independent sources before a claim leaves your notes.\n- **Belief creep.** Studying a system is not joining it. Keep the scholar's bracket: you can map a cosmology precisely while suspending assent. Losing the bracket turns analysis into apologetics.\n- **Etymology as evidence.** Word origins are interesting and prove almost nothing about historical claims. 'The word X comes from Y' is a linguistic fact, not a historical argument about Hermetic Foundations.\n- **Skipping the primary source.** Summaries of summaries drift. Read the actual passage \u2014 even in translation \u2014 before you argue about what a Hermetic Foundations text says."
        }
      ],
