      "handsOnLab": {
        "id": "il01-dee-enochian--lab",
        "title": "Source-tiering dossier: Dee and the Enochian System",
        "brief": "You will build a tiered evidence dossier for Dee and the Enochian System: selecting claims from the module, assigning each an explicit epistemic tier, and grounding every tier in primary sources. This turns passive reading into scholarly practice.",
        "steps": [
          "Select three substantive claims from this module's notes on Dee and the Enochian System.",
          "For each claim, locate the primary source it rests on (the actual text, in translation if needed) and record the exact passage.",
          "Assign each claim a tier: DOCUMENTED, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS, TRADITIONAL, or CONTESTED \u2014 with a one-paragraph justification for the tier.",
          "Find one credible source that disagrees with or complicates each claim; summarize the counter-position fairly.",
          "Write a 400-word synthesis: what survives tiering, what doesn't, and what remains genuinely unknown.",
          "Peer-review your own dossier after 48 hours: flag every place where belief crept past evidence and correct it."
        ],
        "deliverable": "tiering-dossier.md containing the three tiered claims with primary-source passages, counter-positions, and the synthesis \u2014 every claim tiered, every tier justified.",
        "checkpoint": "Three claims, each with a tier, a primary-source passage, a counter-position, and a tier justification; the synthesis names what remains unknown."
      },
      "id": "il01-dee-enochian",
      "mockTask": {
        "id": "il01-dee-enochian--mock",
        "type": "choice",
        "q": "Kelly's role?",
        "choices": [
          "financier",
          "rival",
          "scryer \u2014 Dee recorded",
          "scribe"
        ],
        "answer": 2,
        "explain": "The division of labor: Kelly saw, Dee wrote \u2014 it shapes how we read the diaries. (Concepts: Kelly; Hands-on procedure, step 5.)"
      },
      "notes": [
        {
          "title": "Concepts",
          "body": "- **John Dee (1527\u20131608/09)** \u2014 court mathematician, navigator, and advisor; the biographical record is DOCUMENTED throughout.\n- **\u2018Brittany\u2019** \u2014 Dee\u2019s imperial-imagination concept; the British Empire idea carries his fingerprints.\n- **Mortlake** \u2014 Dee\u2019s library, the largest in England; his power was bibliographic before it was anything else.\n- **Enochian alphabet** \u2014 21 letters, received 6 May 1583; the date is DOCUMENTED, the reception narrative TRADITIONAL.\n- **Angelical Keys** \u2014 the 48 (or 49) Calls; study their structure \u2014 the grammar claims \u2014 before their content.\n- **The diaries (1581/82\u20131589)** \u2014 the day-by-day record; genuine 16th-century documents by SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS.\n- **Kelly** \u2014 the scryer; Dee recorded. The division of labor shapes how the diaries must be read.\n- **Scrying** \u2014 the working practice of the partnership; the diaries are its logbook.\n- **Monas Hieroglyphica (1564)** \u2014 the glyph synthesizing geometry, alchemy, and astronomy; read it as a diagram that thinks.\n- **True and Faithful Relation (1659)** \u2014 Casaubon\u2019s hostile edition of the diaries; the attack that preserved them.\n- **Hostile transmission** \u2014 Casaubon\u2019s irony: the debunker preserved what he attacked; hostile transmission is still transmission.\n- **Yates** \u2014 the 20th-century recovery of Dee; the \u2018Hermetic tradition\u2019 thesis, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS with live CONTESTED edges.\n- **Commonplace book** \u2014 the early-modern method: copy the striking passage, note the tier, add your question.\n- **Tiered reading journal** \u2014 every session logged with what you read, what tier it is, and the evidence; the assessment artifact."
        },
        {
          "title": "Hands-on procedure",
          "body": "Dee\u2019s diaries reward the slow reader. This procedure turns one dated entry into a tiered journal session \u2014 the same move you\u2019ll repeat across the corpus.\n\n1. **Select one dated entry** from the 1581/82\u20131589 diaries \u2014 a short one; the boring entries are often the most DOCUMENTED.\n2. **Copy the entry into your commonplace book** verbatim, with its date and archive reference.\n3. **List the entry\u2019s atomic claims:** who was present, what was seen or received, what was recorded.\n4. **Tier each claim separately:** the entry\u2019s existence and date are DOCUMENTED; the reception narrative is TRADITIONAL; interpretations of what happened are CONTESTED.\n5. **Note the division of labor** in this entry \u2014 what Kelly saw versus what Dee wrote \u2014 and how it shapes your reading.\n6. **Write one open question** the entry leaves you with, and one Monas theorem or passage it echoes, if any.\n7. **Log the session** in your tiered journal: claim, tier, evidence, question. Coverage beats cherry-picking \u2014 read the boring parts."
        },
        {
          "title": "Project brief",
          "body": "The brief. Build the Dee dossier \u2014 three parts, one standard: the document is tiered separately from its content.\n\nDeliverables. (1) A documented timeline of Dee\u2019s life \u2014 mathematician, navigator, advisor, Mortlake \u2014 with a source for every event: letters, library catalogues, dated works. (2) A tiered reading of one Monas theorem or one Angelical Key: structure first (what the figure or the Call\u2019s grammar claims), content second, every claim tiered. (3) A reception comparison: Casaubon\u2019s 1659 True and Faithful Relation against Yates\u2019s 20th-century recovery \u2014 what each preserves, what each argues, and what tier each of their claims earns.\n\nThe standard. The diaries\u2019 genuineness is SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS; what they record is the open question \u2014 tier the document separately from its content, or the dossier fails. No belief is required or tested; precision is."
        },
        {
          "title": "Honesty note",
          "body": "Dee the mathematician and Dee the magus are the same man \u2014 the split is a modern invention, and this module refuses it. \u2018Received 6 May 1583\u2019 is a DOCUMENTED date from the record; the reception narrative around it is TRADITIONAL; what the alphabet is remains CONTESTED \u2014 we keep those three apart at all times. The diaries are genuine 16th-century documents; genuineness is a fact about the paper, not about the angels. Original study material throughout; Interstitium Labs is affiliated with no order and confers no credential; assessments test whether you can read, tier, and argue \u2014 never what you affirm."
        },
        {
          "title": "Worked example: Dee's imperial concept?",
          "body": "**Problem.** Dee's imperial concept?\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud \u2014 in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** 'Brittany' \u2014 the British Empire idea\n- **Why:** Dee advised on the imperial imagination \u2014 DOCUMENTED advisory role. (Concepts: \u2018Brittany\u2019.)\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Dee advised on the imperial imagination \u2014 DOCUMENTED advisory role.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test \u2014 if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
        },
        {
          "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
          "body": "Esoteric material punishes sloppy epistemics harder than most subjects. These are the failure modes that separate scholars from storytellers in Dee and the Enochian System:\n- **Collapsing the tiers.** The single most common failure: treating a TRADITIONAL claim as DOCUMENTED because it feels old. Every claim you touch in Dee and the Enochian System gets an explicit tier \u2014 DOCUMENTED, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS, TRADITIONAL, or CONTESTED \u2014 before you use it.\n- **Anachronism.** Reading modern meanings back into period terms. When a Dee and the Enochian System source says 'spirit', 'energy', or 'science', find what the word meant to the author, not what it means on social media.\n- **Single-source trust.** One grimoire, one translation, one blogger is not a consensus. Corroborate across at least two independent sources before a claim leaves your notes.\n- **Belief creep.** Studying a system is not joining it. Keep the scholar's bracket: you can map a cosmology precisely while suspending assent. Losing the bracket turns analysis into apologetics.\n- **Etymology as evidence.** Word origins are interesting and prove almost nothing about historical claims. 'The word X comes from Y' is a linguistic fact, not a historical argument about Dee and the Enochian System.\n- **Skipping the primary source.** Summaries of summaries drift. Read the actual passage \u2014 even in translation \u2014 before you argue about what a Dee and the Enochian System text says."
        }
      ],
