        "Tier 2 \u2014 scholarly consensus",
        "Tier 3 \u2014 practitioner claim",
        "Tier 4 \u2014 popular myth"
      ],
      "answer": 2,
      "explain": "The diaries record Kelley scrying and Dee transcribing \u2014 the *record* is Tier 1, but the claim that an angel dictated it is the practitioner's own emic account: Tier 3 until corroborated."
    },
    {
      "id": "drill-il01-res2",
      "title": "Hermetic timeline",
      "prompt": "Which figure translated the Corpus Hermeticum into Latin in 1471, sparking the Renaissance Hermetic revival?",
      "choices": [
        "John Dee",
        "Marsilio Ficino",
        "Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa",
        "Giordano Bruno"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explain": "Ficino's 1471 translation (commissioned by Cosimo de' Medici) put the Hermetica before Renaissance readers; Dee, Agrippa, and Bruno all come later."
    },
    {
      "id": "drill-il01-res3",
      "title": "Enochian source criticism",
      "prompt": "What is the 'single-witness problem' in the Enochian diaries?",
      "choices": [
        "Only one manuscript copy survives",
        "Only Kelley scryed; Dee recorded, so every spirit communication passes through one medium",
        "Only one of the 49 Calls was ever published",
        "Casaubon destroyed the second witness's testimony"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explain": "Dee never claimed to see the angels himself \u2014 Kelley was the scryer and Dee the scribe, so the entire corpus rests on Kelley's mediumship."
    },
