  "cheatSheets": [
    {
      "title": "IL-01 Epistemic Tiers Quick Reference",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "h": "DOCUMENTED",
          "body": "Primary sources, artifacts, dated texts \u2014 the bedrock."
        },
        {
          "h": "SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS",
          "body": "What historians agree on \u2014 provisional, cited."
        },
        {
          "h": "TRADITIONAL",
          "body": "Lineage claims \u2014 reported, not endorsed."
        },
        {
          "h": "CONTESTED",
          "body": "Open questions, rival readings \u2014 where the seminar lives."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "IL-01 Key Dates & Figures",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "h": "Dates",
          "body": "1564 Monas \u00b7 1581-89 diaries \u00b7 1583 alphabet \u00b7 1614 Casaubon \u00b7 1659 TFR \u00b7 1888-1903 GD \u00b7 1971-73 Cybersyn"
        },
        {
          "h": "Figures",
          "body": "Hermes Trismegistus (attributed) \u00b7 Dee & Kelly \u00b7 Paracelsus \u00b7 Crowley \u00b7 Spare \u00b7 Carroll \u00b7 Beer \u00b7 Land"
        },
        {
          "h": "Texts",
          "body": "Corpus Hermeticum \u00b7 Asclepius \u00b7 Book of the Law \u00b7 Principia Discordia \u00b7 Monas Hieroglyphica"
        },
        {
          "h": "Method",
          "body": "Tier every claim. Literacy, never belief. Commonplace book + tiered journal."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Tier discipline \u2014 documented vs. claimed (quick reference)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "h": "Tier 1 \u2014 Documented fact",
          "body": "Primary text, dated manuscript, archaeological find, or peer-reviewed history. Cite the artifact: shelfmark, edition, date."
        },
        {
          "h": "Tier 2 \u2014 Scholarly consensus",
          "body": "What specialists currently hold, with named disagreements. E.g. Dee's diaries exist (British Library); their *meaning* is disputed."
        },
        {
          "h": "Tier 3 \u2014 Practitioner claim",
          "body": "What a tradition says about itself. Record emically (in its own logic) without endorsing. Lineage claims live here until corroborated."
        },
        {
          "h": "Tier 4 \u2014 Popular myth",
          "body": "Film, forum, and paperback lore. Useful as reception history; never as evidence. Always label it."
        },
        {
          "h": "The sorting question",
          "body": "For every claim ask: **who wrote it, when, and what did they have to gain?** Then assign a tier before you argue."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Hermetic current \u2014 one-page timeline",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "h": "Alexandria (c. 100\u2013300 CE)",
          "body": "Corpus Hermeticum and Asclepius composed in Graeco-Egyptian milieu. Technical Hermetica (astrology, alchemy, magic) circulate alongside the philosophical dialogues."
        },
        {
          "h": "Renaissance recovery (1460\u20131500s)",
          "body": "Ficino translates the Corpus Hermeticum (1471). Yates' thesis: Hermetic prisca theologia shapes Renaissance magic. Frances Yates, *Giordano Bruno and the Hermetic Tradition* (1964)."
        },
        {
          "h": "Dee & early modern (1500s\u20131600s)",
          "body": "John Dee: Monas Hieroglyphica (1564), Enochian workings with Edward Kelley (1582\u20131589). Rosicrucian manifestos (1614\u20131616)."
        },
        {
          "h": "Golden Dawn (1888\u20131903)",
          "body": "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn systematizes ritual magic: grades, Tarot correspondences, Enochian tablets. Crowley carries the current into Thelema."
        },
        {
          "h": "Chaos magic (1970s\u2013present)",
          "body": "Spare, Carroll, Sherwin: belief as tool, sigilization, paradigm shifting. The CCRU (1990s Warwick) reframes it through cybernetics and hyperstition."
        },
        {
          "h": "Reading rule",
          "body": "Continuity is *claimed* by practitioners; discontinuity is *documented* by historians. Hold both: emic narrative vs. etic evidence."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Dee's corpus \u2014 what survives and where",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "h": "Monas Hieroglyphica (1564)",
          "body": "Dee's glyph unifying astronomy, alchemy, and geometry. Dedicated to Maximilian II. Survives in print; facsimiles widely available."
        },
        {
          "h": "The Enochian diaries (1582\u20131589)",
          "body": "Spirit diaries recording the angel workings with Edward Kelley: the 49 Tables, the Great Table, the Calls. Manuscripts in the British Library (Sloane MSS)."
        },
        {
          "h": "The Heptarchia and Liber Loagaeth",
          "body": "Heptarchia Mystica: the 49 good angels of the days. Liber Loagaeth (Book of Speech from God): 49 tables of letter-squares, never fully 'translated' in Dee's lifetime."
        },
        {
          "h": "Reception",
          "body": "Casaubon's *True & Faithful Relation* (1659) prints the diaries \u2014 framing Dee as deluded. Modern scholarship (Clulee, Woolley, Parry) reads Dee as serious natural philosopher."
        },
        {
          "h": "Source criticism",
          "body": "Kelley is the scryer; Dee the recorder. Every Enochian claim passes through Kelley's mediumship \u2014 note the single-witness problem."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "CCRU concepts \u2014 hyperstition, numogram, Cybersyn",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "h": "Hyperstition",
          "body": "Fiction that makes itself real: stories, diagrams, and code that bootstrap their own conditions of truth. The CCRU's signature concept (cf. Land, Fisher, the CCRU writings)."
        },
        {
          "h": "The numogram",
          "body": "A diagrammatic engine of decimal digits 0\u20139 arranged in zones and currents; used by the CCRU as a divinatory-computational system. Treat as documented artifact of 1990s Warwick theory-fiction."
        },
        {
          "h": "Cybersyn",
          "body": "Chile 1971\u201373: Stafford Beer's cybernetic economic management system \u2014 telex network, operations room, Viable System Model. Documented history (Medina, *Cybernetic Revolutionaries*, 2011), not myth."
        },
        {
          "h": "Cybernetics lineage",
          "body": "Wiener (1948) \u2192 Beer (management cybernetics) \u2192 CCRU (theory-fiction). Each step is traceable in print; the CCRU's *use* of it is the novel move."
        },
        {
          "h": "Tier check",
          "body": "Cybersyn: Tier 1\u20132 (archives, scholarship). Numogram: Tier 1 as artifact, Tier 3 as efficacy claim. Hyperstition: Tier 2 as concept, Tier 4 when invoked as magic."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "answerBank": [
    {
      "q": "Do I have to believe any of this?",
      "a": "No \u2014 assessments test literacy, never belief. The tier system exists precisely so you can study claims rigorously without affirming them. Read, tier, argue.",
      "ref": "il01-hermetic-foundations"
    },
    {
      "q": "Why study esotericism in a tech academy?",
      "a": "Historiography, primary-source literacy, and the tier discipline transfer everywhere \u2014 including to evaluating technical claims. Plus: the history of computing (Dee, cybernetics) runs through this material.",
      "ref": "il01-dee-enochian"
    },
    {
      "q": "Isn't the CCRU just internet mysticism?",
      "a": "The seminar treats it as texts with a documented context (Warwick, 1990s) and a CONTESTED theory (hyperstition). Read the corpus, tier the claims, argue the status \u2014 that's the work.",
      "ref": "il01-ccru-seminar"
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do I start reading?",
      "a": "The Asclepius (complete, short), then Dee's biography, then the Land/CCRU corpus in reading order. Commonplace book from day one.",
      "ref": "il01-hermetic-foundations"
    },
    {
      "q": "What's the journal for?",
      "a": "It's the assessment artifact: every reading logged with tier, evidence, and your open question. It demonstrates the method \u2014 which is the actual skill being taught.",
      "ref": "il01-dee-enochian"
    },
    {
      "q": "How do TRADITIONAL claims get treated?",
      "a": "Reported accurately, labeled honestly \u2014 never endorsed, never mocked. 'The lineage claims X' is a DOCUMENTED fact about the claim, whatever X's own tier is.",
      "ref": "il01-hermetic-foundations"
    }
  ],
